{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Korah took.</b> This pericah is well expounded in Midrash Rabbi Tanchuma." "<b>And Korah took.</b> He took himself to one side, to be set apart from the assembly, to stir up contention over the priesthood. And this is what Onkelos translated: ״וְאִתְפְּלֵג״ — he separated from the rest of the assembly to take hold of strife. So too ״מַה יִּקָּחֲךָ לִבֶּךָ״ (Job 15) — takes you to set you apart from the rest of mankind. Another interpretation: <b>And Korah took</b> — he drew the heads of the sanhedrin among them with words, as it says ״קַח אֶת אַהֲרֹן״ (Leviticus 8), ״קְחוּ עִמָּכֶם דְּבָרִים״ (Hosea 14)." "<b>Son of Yitzhar son of Kehat son of Levi.</b> And it does not mention ״son of Jacob,״ because he pleaded for mercy upon himself that his name not be recalled in connection with their strife, as it says ״בִּקְהָלָם אַל תֵּחַד כְּבֹדִי״ (Genesis 49). And where is his name recalled in connection with Korah? In the genealogy of the duchan in Chronicles, as it says (1 Chronicles 6) ״son of Eviasaf son of Korah son of Yitzhar son of Kehat son of Levi son of Israel.״" "<b>Datan and Aviram.</b> Because the tribe of Reuven was encamped in the south, neighbor to Kehat and his sons who encamp in the south, they joined with Korah in his strife — ״Woe to the wicked, woe to his neighbor.״ And what did Korah see to contend with Moses? He was incensed over the princehood of Elitzafan son of Uzziel, whom Moses had appointed prince over the sons of Kehat by the word of the Omnipresent. Korah said: my father's brothers were four, as it says (Exodus 6) ״And the sons of Kehat״ etc. Amram the firstborn — his two sons took greatness, one a king and one a High Priest. Who is fit to take the second? Is it not I, being the son of Yitzhar, who is second to Amram? And he appointed as prince the son of the youngest of them all! I will contend against him and nullify his words. What did he do? He arose and assembled 250 heads of the sanhedrin — most of them from the tribe of Reuven his neighbors, and they are Elitzur son of Shedeur and his companions and those like them, as it says ״princes of the assembly, those called to the meeting,״ and further on it says (Numbers 1) ״these are the called ones of the assembly.״ And he dressed them in cloaks that were entirely tekhelet. They came and stood before Moses. They said to him: a cloak that is entirely of tekhelet — is it obligated in tzitzit or exempt? He said to them: it is obligated. They began to laugh at him: Is it possible? A cloak of another kind — one thread of tekhelet exempts it; this one, which is entirely tekhelet, should not exempt itself? (Tanchuma)." "<b>The sons of Reuven.</b> Datan and Aviram and On son of Pelet."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:11:18.095112796Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}